# Benchmarks, 2026-11-24: stage c, native code for loops

<!-- SPDX-License-Identifier: CC-BY-4.0 -->

The 25 programs of [`bench/compute`](../bench/compute/), each written in CigScript and again in Python (all 25), JavaScript (24: all but `csv`), Lua (21) and Rust (8), timed side by side on one machine, with one side that is new: **a stage `c` program**, which [cigcompile](https://github.com/otmof-ops/cig-compile) builds with native code for the loops and functions it finds faster that way.

**This is a run of cig's source, not of a release.** Stage `c`'s native code for loops is on `main` and in no release yet, so cig is built here from commit `277e0e2` and cigcompile from commit `be2a18e`, cig as its release workflow builds it for Linux (`cargo build --release --target x86_64-unknown-linux-musl`). It is also another machine than the run of [2026-11-15](BENCHMARKS-2026-11-15.md), which measured cig 1.24.0 as released: a time in one paper is not to be set beside a time in the other. Within this one, every side ran in the same rounds.

Every number of the comparison is made from the samples in [`bench/results/2026-11-24/`](../bench/results/2026-11-24/) by [`bench/compare/report.py`](../bench/compare/report.py), and made again by [`bench/compare/check.py`](../bench/compare/check.py), a second program written from the [specification](../bench/compare/SPEC.md) alone; the two agree on every one.

## In short

- **Where a program has native code, it takes {{geomean.native.stage-c.stage-b}} of its stage `b` time** ({{inverse.native.stage-c.stage-b}} times as fast), at the geometric mean of the {{n.native.stage-c.stage-b}} compute programs cigcompile gave any. The loops of arithmetic gain most: `mandel` takes {{ratio.mandel.stage-c.stage-b}} of its stage `b` time, `fib` {{ratio.fib.stage-c.stage-b}}, `fannkuch` {{ratio.fannkuch.stage-c.stage-b}}, `loop` {{ratio.loop.stage-c.stage-b}}, `sieve` {{ratio.sieve.stage-c.stage-b}}.
- **Over all {{n.compute.stage-c.stage-b}} compute programs a stage `c` program takes {{geomean.compute.stage-c.stage-b}} of stage `b`'s time**: faster on {{faster.compute.stage-c.stage-b}}, level (within 3%) on {{level.compute.stage-c.stage-b}}, slower on {{slower.compute.stage-c.stage-b}}. Most programs get no native code: their loops read a map, build a string or call a function every turn, and the compiler leaves a loop to the bytecode when native code would have to hand every other instruction back. Such a program is its stage `b` program.
- **Against the other languages, built at stage `c`:** {{geomean.compute.stage-c.python}} of CPython 3.12's time (faster on {{faster.compute.stage-c.python}} of {{n.compute.stage-c.python}} programs; stage `b` takes {{geomean.compute.stage-b.python}}), {{geomean.compute.stage-c.lua}} times Lua 5.4's time (stage `b`: {{geomean.compute.stage-b.lua}}), {{geomean.compute.stage-c.luajit-off}} times LuaJIT's interpreter's, {{geomean.compute.stage-c.luajit}} times LuaJIT's with its compiler on, and {{geomean.compute.stage-c.rust}} times Rust's over the {{n.compute.stage-c.rust}} programs Rust has (stage `b`: {{geomean.compute.stage-b.rust}}). On the loops it compiles it is with Lua 5.4 or ahead of it: `mandel` takes {{median.mandel.stage-c}} ms against Lua's {{median.mandel.lua}}, `fannkuch` {{median.fannkuch.stage-c}} against {{median.fannkuch.lua}}, `fib` {{median.fib.stage-c}} against {{median.fib.lua}} and `loop` {{median.loop.stage-c}} against {{median.loop.lua}}. Node 18 takes {{median.hello.node}} ms to start on this machine, which decides its row ({{geomean.compute.stage-c.node}}).
- **A stage `b` program takes {{geomean.compute.stage-b.cig}} of `cig run`'s time**, and a stage `c` program {{geomean.compute.stage-c.cig}}.
- **Start-up is unchanged:** `hello` takes {{median.hello.stage-c}} ms as a stage `c` program and {{median.hello.stage-b}} ms as a stage `b` one, where CPython takes {{median.hello.python}} ms and Node {{median.hello.node}} ms.

## Stage c against stage b

Each program's median time as a stage `b` program and as a stage `c` program, the second divided by the first, and what of the program cigcompile gave native code: its top level, so many of its functions, or nothing. The last two rows are the geometric mean of the ratios over every compute program and over the ones with native code, with how many were faster, level (within 3%) and slower.

{{table:native}}

**What gets native code.** `cigcompile build --stage c` compiles a script's bytecode loop by loop to machine code that runs on the same registers cig's VM uses, so native code can sit beside anything: what it has no machine code for, the interpreter runs.

- **A loop of arithmetic** on ints, floats and bools, with a list's elements read and written by an int: `mandel`, `loop`, `fannkuch`, `sieve` and `deltablue`. `richards` and `spectral` have a loop compiled too and come out level: the loop is a small part of what they do.
- **A function on numbers alone**, compiled whole for the argument types it is called with: `fib`.
- **Not yet:** a loop that calls a function each turn (`closures`, `dispatch`, `spectral`, `trees`), reads a map's field (`nbody`, `maps`, `json`), or builds a string (`strings`, `strbuild`, `words`). Native code gains about two nanoseconds an instruction over cig's VM, and going between the two costs several times that, so the compiler weighs each loop and leaves it to the bytecode when it would not come out ahead. By its own figures a stage `c` program is never slower than its stage `b` program; here none was by more than the run's noise: the largest ratios are `json-deep`'s {{ratio.json-deep.stage-c.stage-b}} and `richards`'s {{ratio.richards.stage-c.stage-b}}, and `json-deep` has no native code at all, so its two sides are one program's bytes run in the same rounds.

**What the native code itself adds:** the same stage `c` programs run with `CIG_NATIVE=0`, which makes them run their bytecode and never load their native code, take {{inverse.compute.stage-c.stage-c-off}} times as long at the geometric mean (stage `c` ÷ the same program without its native code: {{geomean.compute.stage-c.stage-c-off}}).

## Against each language

A cell is cig's time divided by the other language's: the geometric mean, over the programs both have, of the ratio of their median times. Under 1, cig took less time. In brackets: how many of those programs cig was faster on, level with (within 3%) and slower on.

{{table:ratios-compute}}

- **`cig run`** is cig's tree-walking interpreter, as `cig run script.cig` runs a script.
- **A stage `a` program** is what `cigcompile build` makes by default: cig itself with the script inside it, run by the same interpreter.
- **A stage `b` program** (`cigcompile build --stage b`) runs the script's bytecode on cig's VM.
- **A stage `c` program** (`cigcompile build --stage c`) is a stage `b` program with native code beside its bytecode.

These are the {{programs.compute}} programs that are not start-up tests; start-up has [its own section](#start-up).

